2026-09-20 -- Idea (Brad): RAM write-detect register for planar-screen emulation (not adopted)
  For ST/MiNT-style software that writes a planar screen directly: a CPLD
  sets one bit per region on any CPU write into a fixed RAM window; the
  vblank ISR reads the bits and converts only dirty regions planar->packed
  into the real 4bpp/2bpp frame.  Suggested shape: fixed 32K window, 8 x 4K
  regions (~25 ST low-res lines each), write-1-to-clear or read-clears so a
  write between read and clear is not lost.  Rough cost without it: a full
  320x200 c2p pass ~1M cycles (estimate, 10-20 cycles/pixel), i.e. all of
  the ~52% CPU a 4bpp screen leaves, for ~8 fields.  Not needed for ST mono
  (1 plane = packed 1bpp) or for software drawing through a packed-pixel VDI
  driver.  Home: GLUE fit doubtful (unmeasured); the ATF1504 socket freed by
  folding TIMING into PIXEL (64 LC, 32 I/O) would hold a bus watcher on rev 2.
  68010 bus-error-and-restart emulation rejected as far too slow per write.

2026-09-20 -- Idea (Brad): LITERAL replaces RUN_COLOR as a one-word pixel (candidate experiment, after the indexed-PIXEL landing)
  Encoding { 2'b00, 2'b11, colour[11:0] }: the RUN_COLOR prefix plus a full
  R4G4B4 colour is exactly one word, so N literal pixels = N words, no count,
  no refetch sequencing.  Ordinary one-slot record: at the 2-clock fetch
  cadence it paints its slot + one HOLD slot = one half-rate pixel; a banked
  pair gives two adjacent 640-res pixels (L5).  CORRECTION same day: HOLD
  lasts only until the next word stages (2 clocks in steady state), so a
  literal is NOT a one-word span of arbitrary length except when nothing
  follows it on the line (right border).  A mid-line flat span in an
  arbitrary colour stays SET cmp_color + RUN (2 words); replacing RUN_COLOR
  therefore gives up one-word spans in the 8 corner colours in exchange for
  true-colour pixels.  cmp_color0/1 stay untouched by literals.
  Sprite format this enables (320-density game modes): each sprite row a
  precompiled word array, one LITERAL per coloured pixel, RUN(passthrough)
  for transparent gaps; a line program is gap RUN + row + gap RUN + row...
  (concatenation, the background being in PIXELS); depth overlap = slicing
  the back row by index.  Rows compiled for a known entry state (preceded by
  a >= 4-px run, because a banked pair lands on consecutive 1-clock slots).
  ~16 words per 16-px row vs 2 for a MASK row; ~100 literal pixels/line over
  a 40-word attr background.  Zero-copy variant: descriptors point at row
  data in place, X = the gap RUN count word; ~14 SYSCLK descriptor overhead
  per fragment.  Proposed implementation:
  the literal loads RGB_OUT from staged_word on its slot and HOLD of a literal
  is "RGB_OUT keeps its value" - no 12-bit latch, and RUN_COLOR's 3-FF colour
  latch is freed.  Cost: one more source term on the 12 RGB_OUT cells plus a
  hold/CE term; COMPOSITOR is 122/128 LC, fit untried.  To pin: MASK over a
  held literal (restore rule), as today's rule for MASK over RUN_COLOR.
  Bandwidth ceiling with PIXELS idle and ~10% CPU (estimate from the cost
  model, 2.44 SYSCLK/word in full descriptors): ~164 literals/line = 160x480
  true-colour full width at 4-clock pixels, or a half-width window at
  half-rate density; within the normal 147-word budget, ~50 exact-colour
  patch pixels per line over a 4bpp photo.  RUN_COLOR users to re-author:
  apps/dtest, apps/smurf + image-tools/sprite2spans, apps/dmabench, the
  griffin-ports Smurf borders, suite case m7-kiosk.  SET target 7 stays the
  palette-write filler.
  Addition (Brad, same day): a counted "hold" record - repeat the last output
  colour for N slots.  LITERAL + HOLD(N) = any-colour span in 2 words with no
  register clobber (restores RUN_COLOR's virtue for all 4096 colours), and
  compresses same-colour runs in literal sprite rows (wins at >= 3 game
  pixels).  Encoding idea: a flag in RUN's unused top count bits (640 slots
  need 10 of the 12), shrinking the counter by 2 FF - terminal/complement
  logic and HOLD framing dependence on those bits unchecked.  No new playback
  state: cur_src's fourth value (today RUN_COLOR) becomes "RGB_OUT keeps its
  value"; LITERAL loads RGB_OUT for one slot then enters it, HOLD(N) enters it
  for N.  Semantics: hold after a MASK repeats the mask's last pixel (same
  repaint rule as MASK over RUN_COLOR today); hold after passthrough smears
  the last playfield pixel.

2026-09-20 -- Audio: run-record voices on looping FIFOs (idea, Brad + Claude; PROPOSAL)
  From the Smurf port on the indexed PIXEL: with the picture path down to
  ~20% of the available CPU, the software SN76489 is 56-61% of it (the
  2026-09-16 numbers, now seen under DMA stretch 1.36), and the run-length
  renderer is already the production path, so there is no software lever
  left of any size.  Rejected on the way: pre-rendered PCM stems (1.1-1.6
  MB for three 8.5 s loops; Brad: a waste of RAM for audio this simple);
  four independently loaded 7200s summed in analog (solves mixing, not
  generation or data size).
  Idea: a voice's FIFO pair holds RUN records {level8, end_of_loop,
  count7} at a ~109 kHz tick; the 7200's retransmit pin replays a short
  record list, so a square wave is a two-record loop costing no CPU; PCM
  is the same record with a short count and no loop; voices summed in
  analog; records routed with the existing AUDIO strobe plus a control
  word on the unused SPARE strobe (reset mask, select mask), no ENGINE
  change.  Known weakness: a volume change restarts the wave (listen
  first).  Alternatives kept in view: a real PSG chip; plain PSG voices
  in a CPLD.  Full text, datasheet items to verify and the ladder:
  audio-run-voices-proposal.md.  Nothing fitted or simulated.
  Addendum, same day (Brad): "Form 2" for symmetry with video -- keep the
  SAMPLES FIFO as is, add an AUDCMD FIFO (SET/WAIT records on the SPARE
  strobe) and a sixth CPLD (ATF1508, MIXER) that reads both and drives the
  DACs.  Preferred sub-form: voice state and wave tables in one of the
  bin's 8Kx8 SRAMs, one phase adder and one saturating accumulator
  time-shared over 8-16 wavetable voices per tick, PCM as the passthrough
  term.  Same file, sections F2.1-F2.4.  Unfitted.

2026-09-24 -- VIDCMD 1-word-per-clock read closed; line-authoring rules of thumb
  Closed form from the 2026-09-03 pinned numbers, measured back from a
  capture edge C: the fall's clock edge <= C-32 (tCO 8 + tA 15 + flight 2
  + tSU 7), the rise's >= C-8 (tCO min 3 assumed + tDV 5, tH 0), the next
  fall >= rise + 15 (tRR 10 + the 5 ns tCO spread): 39 ns per read against
  the 39.72 ns period, 0.7 ns of slack.  General form: an /RE low time L
  gives a guaranteed Q window of L - 10 ns.  Rejected on that arithmetic:
  half-period /RE from both clock edges (either phasing; the 10 ns window
  misses the 40 and 60 ns edges by 7-12 ns), a 74AC00 NAND shaping the
  clock (same waveform with tPD in place of tCO; this was the 2026-08-10
  proposal), a 50.35 MHz quadrature clock (10 ns edge grid), a 2x fetch
  clock, and the ~87%-phase delay element (the 2026-08-18 tolerance
  stack).  Routes that would work: two FIFO pairs read as a 32-bit gang
  (COMPOSITOR is 64/64 I/O, so a sequencer CPLD or a bigger package;
  ENGINE's nSIGNAL_SPARE is already a fourth deposit strobe, so the
  builder could split even/odd records into two descriptors with no
  ENGINE change), or faster parts (IDT7200L12 + ATF1508AS-7, roughly
  14 ns of slack -- the L12 and AS-7 figures have not been read from the
  datasheets).  Not pursued: the fetch rate only sets local pitch, ENGINE
  delivery (~147 words/line) sets the line total, and MASK already paints
  per pixel.  Waveforms: the VIDCMD Fetch Cascade page,
  https://claude.ai/artifact/6pwSZyqXnKi6sfzW7Sa559.
  Correction to the 2026-09-03 wording: "+7.7 setup / +6 hold" are the
  margins; the requirements are tSU 7 + ~2 ns flight before the edge and
  tH 0 after it.
  Authoring rules of thumb (datasheet 6.1, RT1..RT7), from hand derivation
  against compositor.v's fetch rules plus the fetch-engine model (the
  Line Player page's port of vidcmd_plan_line): a RUN/RUN_COLOR of >= 2 px
  ends exactly where authored; a run of >= 3 px always re-arms the banked
  pair (a run of 2 only when the record behind it was already parked);
  a 1-px record is exact inside the pair and 2 px wide outside it (after
  RUN(10): RC1,RUN -> 10,11; RC1 x2 -> 10,11,13; RC1 x3 -> 10,11,13,15);
  repeated 1-px features are exact at 4-px pitch (RUN 3 + feature) and
  stretch at 2-3-px pitch; masks chain gapless, RUN->mask 0,
  mask->SET->mask 2.  compositor_tb re-run 2026-09-24: 179 checks, 0
  failures (the on-disk compositor_sim.log predated the 2026-08-24 mask
  rework and still said 8-slot masks and a 3-slot mask seam).  The
  RUN >= 3 pair re-arm is a model and hand-derivation result, not yet a
  compositor_tb case.

2026-09-26 -- MIXER fit rung (i): sequencer + SRAM port + phase adder + accumulators (measurement)
  wave-table-design.md step 2.  cpld/mixer/mixer.v (module Mixer), rung
  wrappers in cpld/mixer/ladder/, targets `make mixer` (the default
  configuration), `make mixer-ladder` (every wrapper, totals printed) and
  `make mixer-sim` (cpld/mixer/mixer_tb.v).  ATF1508AS PLCC84, -preassign
  ignore, JTAG on, no //PIN block, no griffin.generated.vh.
  What the rung holds: AUDIO_TICK 2-FF synchronized, falling edge latched
  as a pending tick; a 3-SYSCLK SRAM access (nOE or nWE low during t1,t2;
  reads captured at the edge ending t2; nobody drives D during t0, so
  every turnaround has one dead clock); per voice PHASE_LO r, INC_LO r,
  PHASE_LO w, PHASE_HI r, INC_HI r, PHASE_HI w, [CTL r], AMP r -- the
  PHASE byte is read FIRST into `sum`, INC is added to it off the bus, and
  `sum` is the write-back byte, then the sign, so there is no INC staging
  register; `sum` also takes SET_DATA, so the D pins are its macrocells.
  Accumulator add straight off the bus at the AMP capture, acc + (D ^ neg)
  + neg, saturating (so -(-128) = +127).  Address = {voice[3:0], item[2:0]}
  registers, A[12:7] constant 0.  SET stand-in: SET_STROBE edge queues one
  write of SET_DATA at SET_ADDR when idle, a pending tick first.  DAC: one
  shared byte plus two latch clocks (10 pins against 16); in the
  two-accumulator form DAC = acc_r, R is latched, acc_r takes acc_l, L is
  latched (the copy is a load term, no macrocells); in the two-pass and
  mono forms DAC is the accumulator itself.  Latch clocks are one SYSCLK
  wide with the byte stable one SYSCLK before and after the rise.
  Ladder (LC/FF/PT, I/O used of 64 INCLUDING the 4 JTAG pins, fitter pass):
    (i-a)  mono, 8 voices                       95/40/314   55  pass 1 (23 cascades)
    (i-b)  stereo, TWO accumulators + PAN, 8 v  126/51/446  56  pass 2 (cascade)
    (i-c)  (i-b) with 16 voices                 DOES NOT FIT: placement fail
                                                 pass 1, grouping fail 2-4
    (i-b2) stereo, ONE accumulator, two passes  110/43/370  56  pass 1 (29 cascades)
           (pass 0 = the walk into L; pass 1 re-reads PHASE_HI, CTL, AMP
           into the same accumulator for R)
    (i-c2) (i-b2) with 16 voices                122/43/425  56  pass 3
    xor_synthesis = on: (i-b) 126/51/447 pass 2, (i-b2) 110/43/370 pass 1;
      no change on either.
  Levers tried and rejected (each measured; every one worse):
    registered AMP operand (sum <= D ^ neg at the capture, acc add one
      clock later register+register, overlapping the next t0): (i-b)
      128/53/421 pass 2 -- fewer PT, more LC.
    async reset on GCLR instead of the synchronous reset (on top of the
      above; the sync reset is a literal in nearly every equation):
      (i-a) 102/42/371 p2, (i-b) 127/53/448 p2, (i-c) 128/53/389 p1 (FITS,
      barely), (i-b2) 118/45/428 p2, (i-c2) 113/45/412 p2.  Worse on every
      8-voice rung; the two 16-voice rungs improving shows how noisy the
      fitter is at 90%+.
    one adder shared between the phase add and the accumulator add
      (operand mux acc/sum, D ^ (neg & item_amp), cin mux): (i-a)
      115/40/440 p2, (i-b2) 115/43/393 p1 -- the operand mux costs more
      than the second carry chain it removes.
  In every count 6 LC are the constant A[12:7] outputs (the fitter spends
  a 0-PT macrocell on each) and nCE is a 1-PT macrocell: a board with nCE
  tied low and those pins driven only when wavetables (rung v) need them
  is 7 LC cheaper than the numbers above.
  GATE: (i-b) under 100 LC -- FAIL.  Two accumulators is 126; the cheapest
  stereo form is the two-pass (i-b2) at 110 (104 net of the constant
  address pins).  Mono is 95.
  PINS: this rung 52 user pins + 4 JTAG = 56 (SYSCLK on the dedicated
  clock pin; mono 55).  Paper count for the real chip, JTAG on: 35 (nRESET
  on GCLR, AUDIO_TICK, A 13, D 8, nCE/nOE/nWE, DAC 8 + 2) + AUDCMD 16+2 +
  SAMPLES 16+2 + JTAG 4 = 75: FAIL; SAMPLES popped a byte at a time (8+3):
  68: FAIL; AUDCMD on the shared SRAM byte bus (2 /R + EF = 3) and SAMPLES
  16+2: 60: borderline; both FIFOs on the shared byte bus: 45: PASS; the
  DAC byte on that bus as well: 37.  The shared-byte-bus rung is therefore
  mandatory, not optional.
  SYSCLK per tick, 3 per access: mono 170; two accumulators 196 (16
  voices 388); two-pass 268 (16 voices 532); 890 available.  Four clocks
  an access would still fit every form (two-pass 16 voices: 708).
  Bench (mixer_tb.v, 9 ticks, 4 voices, 100 ns behavioural SRAM): DAC
  sequence hand-derived in its header before the first run and matched by
  all three configurations (saturation both ways, the low->high carry, a
  SET between ticks); -DMUTATE=1 (wrapping accumulator) fails at tick 2
  with the predicted 0x7D/0x6D.  The first bench draft had two bench bugs
  (an all-z bus reads as x under reduction XOR; the L latch is the LAST
  event in the two-accumulator form, not the R latch) and no RTL bug.
  Section 2 consequences: (1) the access order above is forced by the
  write-back -- PHASE before INC -- and the layout is unchanged; (2) AMP
  = -128 is legal and -(-128) saturates to +127; (3) MODE, RESET_PHASE,
  WAVE_PAGE are not read in this rung; (4) 16 voices costs 12 LC in the
  two-pass form and does not fit with two accumulators.
  What this means for rungs ii-iv on one ATF1508: WAIT needs a 15-bit
  counter (~15 FF + terminal), the two FIFO read ports ~8-10, the LFSR
  ~17: 110 + ~42 > 128.  Form 2.3 as pinned will not fit one chip with
  everything in it.  Candidates, none tried: (a) WAIT as a 16-bit SRAM
  field walked by the same phase adder (INC = 1 toward the all-ones
  terminal: costs accesses, not macrocells); (b) noise as a 256-byte
  table voice (rung v's lookup) instead of the LFSR; (c) a second CPLD
  for AUDCMD/FIFOs, with the voice engine alone in MIXER; (d) mono
  (95 LC) with pan done in analog.
  For step 3 (datasheets): nWE rises on the same edge the address advances
  (address hold after write = the tCO spread only); the D data outlasts
  nWE's rise by the output-enable path delay; nWE and nOE pulses are two
  SYSCLK (142 ns at 14 MHz), address-to-capture 214 ns minus tCO.

