  wavetable, AUDCMD SET/WAIT with the FIFO ports, all in one ATF1508 at
  pass 1 (128/128) with 44 of 64 pins including JTAG.  With ZERO spare
  macrocells: the LFSR (MODE 1/3) and 15 voices do not fit, RESET_PHASE
  is not implemented, and equivalent RTL variants fail grouping.  Whether
  "no LFSR" is a substantial limitation is Brad's call (Smurf's noise is
  periodic and is a MODE 2 table; white noise is not).
  PINS (real): SYSCLK (GCLK), nRESET (GCLR), AUDIO_TICK, A 13, D 8, nOE,
  nWE, nR x4, nEF x2, DAC 8 + 2 latch clocks = 42 user I/O less the two
  dedicated inputs = 40 + 4 JTAG = 44 (42 with AUDIO_TICK and nEF_AUDCMD
  on pins 2 and 84).  nCE tied low.
  SYSCLK per tick (bench-measured = hand count): reads 3, writes 4; seed
  3 + square voice 26 / wavetable 29 in pass 0, 9 / 12 in pass 1, latch
  2 each pass: 8 square 290, tick 1 of the bench (7 square + 1 table)
  296, 8 tables 338; + 20 for the WAIT walk when waiting; 15 voices 535 /
  625.  The right latch follows the left by 77 (square) to 101 (tables)
  SYSCLK.  AUDCMD: SET 10 SYSCLK (HI r, LO r, w), WAIT 42 (HI r, w, LO
  r, w, w 0x80, w 0x81, walk 20).  Idle gap 890 - tick - walk: 580 with
  8 square voices, 532 with 8 tables.  SETs per boundary (gap minus one
  WAIT, over 10): 53 with 8 square voices, 49 with 8 tables, 20 with 15
  tables; the driver's first-emit (16) is well inside.  Records still in
  the FIFO when the tick is seen slide to the next boundary (the model's
  constant).
  Bench (mixer_tb.v): behavioural MCM60L64P10 (100 ns) with the write
  strobe 8 ns late against A and D (tCOP spread), checking address
  stable through the strobe and 5 ns after (tWR), data valid 40 ns before
  (tDW) and at the strobe (tDH); two 7200L15 pairs (tA/tRHZ/tREF/tWEF
  15, Q high-Z unless /R low, /R on empty is an error); a driver count on
  D at every edge; DAC byte stable a SYSCLK either side of its clock.
  Nine ticks at the real rate with 5 voices (one wavetable reading a
  12-entry table at page 2), 8 records at reset (SETs, WAIT 3, WAIT 0,
  WAIT 2) plus one late SET, three SAMPLES words (seed saturation both
  ways).  Expectations hand-derived in the header from section 2 before
  the first run and matched on the first run by every configuration;
  the only bench-side fix was a declaration order.  MUTATE=1 (wrapping
  accumulator) fails at tick 2, MUTATE=2 (no walk after a WAIT load, WAIT
  n runs n+1) at tick 4, MUTATE=3 (3-clock writes) on the first write:
  data gone at the late strobe and the address moved -- the bench sees
  step 3's failure.  One RTL bug found by the bench: the (ii-wi) lever
  needed bit 15 forced (the record's type bit is 0, ~n's bit 15 is 1).
  Section 2 consequences, for Brad to edit: (1) RESET_PHASE not in rung
  (ii); (2) MODES 1/3 = square until an LFSR fits somewhere; (3) the WAIT
  field owns 0x80..0x87 (INC written by hardware), still >= 0x80; (4) the
  DAC byte is offset binary; (5) the record-format bend (WAIT carries ~n)
  buys 2 cells and is not the default; (6) 15 voices do not fit: 8 is the
  number; (7) SETs-per-boundary bound 49..53 for 8 voices; (8) the right
  latch follows the left by 77..101 SYSCLK, not "about 100".

2026-09-26 -- Audio: MIXER retired at 128/128, Z80 sound processor chosen (decision, Brad)
  Rung (ii) of the CPLD MIXER (entry above) fitted the whole PSG subset
  at exactly 128/128 LC, 44 I/O, pass 1, with no LFSR (MODES 1/3 play
  as square), no 15th voice, no RESET_PHASE, and the same RTL landing
  123-128 or failing grouping on a single literal.  Brad's gate rule
  from wave-table-design.md step 4 was "no limitation he considers
  substantial, else a Z80"; the missing LFSR (PSG games lean on white
  noise for drums and explosions) and the zero headroom (the step-6
  bench and any hardware bug fix would each be a re-fit gamble) were
  judged substantial.  The CPLD's real advantage, 8 stereo voices with
  wavetables against 4-6 mono on a Z80 by hand count, was weighed and
  set aside.
  Decided form: Z80B 6 MHz (bin: 4/6/8 MHz parts), 27C128 program+table
  ROM, ONE 6264 8Kx8 RAM, one ganged 16-bit 7200 pair on the existing
  ENGINE AUDIO_FIFO_W strobe (the rev-1 audio pair's footprint; 7201
  drops in), 74HC138 + half a 74HC139 decode, 4.7k pull-up pack, 2x
  74HC574 DAC latches, AUDIO_TICK -> /NMI, output stage unchanged, the
  CD4049 MSB inverters removed (the Z80 emits offset binary).  One FIFO
  carries everything: SET / WAIT / SAMPLES (inline PCM copied to an
  SRAM ring) / VOICE (table base+length in Z80 RAM) / LOAD+JUMP (a title
  uploads its own program, e.g. an exact SN76489 or AY at the
  register-write level) / RESET.  Encodings in sound/audio_records.h,
  constants AUDIO_REC_* in griffin.yml.
  No status path to the 68000, by design (Brad): no /EF readback, no
  reset bit, no flow control.  ENGINE has no stall input on the strobe,
  so a flag could only report after the fact; deposits are descriptors
  at known lines scheduled against the ROM's documented drain rates; a
  bad upload is an analyzer-and-rebuild event; the system reset is the
  only reset.  Kept because free: a LOAD checksum, and the ROM-owned
  NMI epilogue (the vector is at 0x0066 in ROM) that re-seeds SP, counts
  overruns and falls back to the ROM synth.
  No /EF input to the Z80 either.  IDT7200 DSC-2679/7 "Read Enable":
  "When all data has been read from the FIFO, the Empty Flag (EF) will
  go low, allowing the 'final' read cycle but inhibiting further read
  operations with the data outputs remaining in a high impedance state.
  ... When the FIFO is empty, the internal read pointer is blocked from
  R so external changes in R will not affect the FIFO when it is empty."
  So a pulled-up bus reads 0xFF on an empty read; SET address 0x7F is
  reserved to keep 0xFF unambiguous; the FIFO high-byte port is 0xFF so
  the operand fetch before the IN leaves the bus at all ones.  4.7k:
  ~120 ns to TTL high on 50 pF against ~250 ns from /RD to sample at
  6 MHz, and 1 mA through the EPROM's outputs (1k would exceed the
  27C128's ~2 mA sink).  Rejected for /EF: 74HC125 read port (a chip);
  Z80 /INT via an inverter (a chip); SIO DCD in RR0 bit 3 (~22 T per
  poll with the Reset External/Status quirk; would have needed no CTC
  with TxC = the 6 MHz CPU clock in x64 mode = 93,750 baud, exact on an
  FTDI) -- all moot once the sentinel was verified.
  Debug: a passive 12-pin header (/WR, /IORQ, DEBUG select, D0..D7,
  GND) decoded by a Pico 2 or a logic analyzer.  A bit-bang TX bit would
  have needed a latch chip; the SIO+CTC pair was rejected as "a whole
  computer".  Two bits into PORTS pins were rejected because the 68000
  would have to poll at the bit rate (the PS/2 lesson).
  Memory: 8 KB only, deliberately period-plausible; a 62256 fits the
  same socket with a jumper if uploads and samples ever crowd it.  DRAM
  (Mirage-class sample memory, Z80 refresh) noted as a rev-3 stretch and
  not pursued: the RAS/CAS timing chain is exactly the unknown the Z80
  was chosen to avoid.
  Napkin budget, to be replaced by sound/README.md's measurements: a
  tick is 381 T at 6 MHz (508 at 8); a wavetable voice ~80-100 T, a
  square voice ~130 T stereo saturating, PCM ring copy ~42 T per pair
  plus ~30 T per tick to pop and add, overhead ~100 T.  Expect 3 voices
  + PCM comfortable at 6 MHz, 4 voices without PCM tight, 4 + PCM only
  at 8 MHz.  Smurf needs 4 (3 tones + noise).  The feasibility test's
  gate: 4 voices + PCM within 381 T, or 4 voices without PCM with 15 %
  headroom.
  Package split: this package = decision, docs, griffin.yml, the
  feasibility test.  Package B = PORTS drops the rev-1 pop logic,
  emulator runs the ROM image on a z80emu core, smurf and firmware
  deposit records.  Rev-1 AUDIO_CONTROL/AUDIO_STATUS stay in the YAML
  marked rev-1-only until then so every consumer keeps building.

