  15,734 Hz needs roughly 560-700 T per tick with the best Z80 code
  (84/voice + 130-226 framing), which no part in the bin reaches: the
  Z80B has 381, the Z80H 508.  A 20 MHz Z84C0020 (1271 T) would carry
  it with headroom but is a 1990s part.  Open for Brad: the Z80 as a
  sequencer/PCM/upload processor in front of a real PSG (AY-3-8910 /
  YM2149 / SN76489, the 1984 arcade sound-board form; the AY's two
  8-bit ports are a free debug port), a faster Z80, a 68010/68008 sound
  CPU, or the retired 128/128 CPLD.

2026-09-27 -- Audio: Z80 ROM v2 levers after the v1 measurement (decision, Brad)
  v1 (entry above) matched the model bit for bit at 1263 T per tick;
  Brad kept 15,734 Hz (his own experiments ruled out a lower rate) and
  chose these changes, all to be measured on the same harness:
  1. Minimal NMI: accept, EX AF,AF' + EXX, JP through a patched operand
     (reset points it at the ROM tick), the tick, EXX + EX AF,AF', RETN.
     No supervisor (stack re-seed, re-entry flag, overrun fallback): the
     emulator is the development tool; a bad upload is a rebuild.
  2. Tick assembled from templates at CONFIGURE time: voice count, mode
     and side per voice, global mono, PCM on/off, all baked into
     straight-line code; no dispatch, no loop.  CONFIGURE = point the JP
     at a hold stub (the NMI cannot be masked), rebuild, point it back.
  3. SET patches immediates in the live tick with single-instruction
     stores (INC 16-bit, AMP/page byte, PHASE word); structural fields
     wait for CONFIGURE.  Drain cost per SET ~30-60 T against 301 mean
     / 4268 max measured for v1.
  4. SAMPLES FIFO pair restored on AUDIO_FIFO_W (the rev-1 footprint),
     AUDCMD pair on SPARE.  PCM popped in the tick (~35 T mono / 60
     stereo); the v1 ring copy cost ~290 T per pair in the drain, more
     than a 6 MHz gap holds, so this is required, not optional.  Hold
     the last pair on empty.  SAMPLES record and ring removed.
  5. 74HC244 status port: /EF and /HF of both pairs.  Removes the 0xFF
     sentinel and its author-facing warts (left sample -1, reserved SET
     address 0x7F, one LOAD byte per word) and the pull-up pack; /HF
     lets the drain batch 128 records blind.
  6. Mix law unchanged (per-add saturation, the 11 T offset-binary
     trick) so the bit-exact oracle stands; 16-bit accumulate with a
     clamp at the end was costed at ~+20 T per stereo voice and set
     aside; budgeted 8-bit adds would save ~6 T per voice.
  7. Register-resident phases for the voices the alternate register set
     and IX/IY can hold; ~43-51 T per voice against 71-79 with the
     phase in a RAM word (all-immediates template: LD HL,(phase) 16 /
     LD DE,#inc 10 / ADD 11 / LD (phase),HL 16 / LD L,H 4 / LD H,#page
     7 / ADD A,(HL) 7).
  8. Side baked into the add target (one add per voice); mono = one
     accumulator to both DACs.  Period note: 1984 hardware panned by
     enable bits (YM2151) or not at all (Amiga hard L/R); per-voice L/R
     levels arrived with the PC Engine (1987).
  9. DUMP record writes a state snapshot to the debug header.
  Estimate, explicitly the third and to be replaced by measurement:
  Smurf 4 voices mono + PCM ~381 T at 6 MHz (no headroom), 3 voices +
  PCM ~310 (18 %), 4 voices at 8 MHz ~75 %.  The gate decides Z80B vs
  Z80H.  Plan after the gate: griffin.yml and docs for the settled
  protocol, emulator runs rom.bin on the z80emu core in place of the
  PORTS audio model (rev-1 AUDIO_CONTROL writes become no-ops until the
  PORTS re-fit), Smurf port on records in another session.

