2026-09-29 -- Video: sprite lines from static pieces, delivered a line ahead (finding, super-engine m11)
  Lesson carried over from the audio work (deposits by reference into the
  FIFO, ahead of their line): a sprite line's VIDCMD program can be FIVE
  descriptors pointing at read-only data -- a 2-word cap (SET pix_base +
  left border), a gap word from a 640-entry RUN(pass,n) table, the pose row's
  words, a tail word from the same table, the right border -- instead of 21
  words the CPU copies into an arena.  The extra descriptor turn-around
  (+40 SYSCLK a line) breaks the one-hblank deadline, so the pieces are
  deposited k lines EARLY into the VIDCMD FIFO; the model (m11, hand-derived
  to the SYSCLK) is pixel-identical at k = 1..8, k = 0 fails as derived.
  COMPOSITOR semantics are positional, not temporal (vidcmd-spec §4.2), the
  one exception being /RS, which empties the FIFO: the bootstrap lines go in
  the vblank preamble after the pulse.  Lookahead is a uniform pipeline
  offset for the whole frame, not per sprite.  Pads never framed anything;
  the fixed 21-word shape is unnecessary once descriptor count is per line.
  Side finding: the Smurf packet derivation 20+2W+15 <= 78 is conservative
  by seven words (edma3 pre-pays the parked descriptor's fetch): W <= 28.
  For the Smurf port this removes the word arena, its tear, and most of the
  composer (3 pointer stores a sprite line); overlap lines keep a small
  scratch program.  Write-up: super-engine/STATIC-PIECES.md.

2026-09-29 -- AUDIO: offset-binary samples, left channels on D[15:8] (decision)
  Question: DMA to the sample FIFOs carried the low byte only, so a stereo
  interleaved buffer had to be de-interleaved into two mono buffers, and
  D[7:0] carried nine 7200 loads.  Brad asked whether the right channels
  could sit on D[15:8] and whether the byte could be chosen at push time.
  Chosen: channels 0 and 1 (left) have their 7200 data pins on D[15:8];
  channels 2 and 3 (right) and the control FIFO stay on D[7:0].  Left goes
  high because an 8-bit stereo WAV data chunk is L,R,L,R with L at even
  offsets, i.e. in the 68000 word's high byte, so a WAV chunk streams to a
  left and a right channel as two descriptors over the same words with no
  reshuffle.  A mono table is authored with the sample in both bytes; that
  is free because every sample already occupied a word with one byte
  discarded.  Loading: D[7:0] seven 7200 loads (was nine), D[15:8] six
  (was four); the issue entry stays open (the review accepted six).
  Push-time lane selection rejected: ENGINE has no spare code (0..7 used,
  64/64 pins), and a per-channel select bit in AUDIO plus two 74HC157 per
  steered channel buys nothing over the doubled byte.  AUDCTL could move
  up too (six and seven) if the loading review wants it; not done.
  Samples are OFFSET BINARY again, 0x80 = silence (AUDIO_SILENCE), reversing
  the 2026-09-27 return to two's complement.  Reason: the 74HC04 on Q7
  disappears (one chip and, with it, the pull resistor its CMOS input
  needed against a floating Q; a bare R-2R ladder on high-Z Q is harmless
  because the 74HC4066 is open whenever /EF is asserted).  Cognitive cost
  judged small: every direct-ladder machine of the era was unsigned
  (Covox, Mac, Genesis DAC, IIgs Ensoniq), 8-bit WAV is unsigned, and the
  68000 never mixes or scales in this design, so the bias appears only
  where tables are built (v + 128).
  Rate error for common file rates (step 1,573,437.5 Hz): 11,025 -> PERIOD
  142 = 11,003 Hz (-3.5 cents); 8,000 -> 196 = 7,987 Hz (-2.8 cents);
  22,050 -> 70 = 22,160 Hz (+8.7 cents, audible against a reference;
  resample offline).  Duration drifts by the same fraction.
  Done: griffin.yml (AUDIO comment, part list, AUDIO_SILENCE 0x80, Stream
  FIFO parts, Audio output stage, loading issue); super-engine/descriptor.h
  lane helpers (audio_channel_byte, audio_sample_word, audio_stereo_word,
  audio_control_word, audio_sample_from_signed; audio_byte_word removed);
  audio_chip.h SILENCE 0x80, AnalogRender subtracts the bias; suite author
  writes one interleaved stereo buffer (audio RAM halves) and both channel
  descriptors read the same words; emulator picks the lane by channel,
  self-test tables biased; psg_channels HIGH/LOW 0xFF/0x01; apps/smurf demo
  stores v + 128 in both bytes and fills silence with 0x8080; engine.v
  comment; griffin.md, reminders.md, audio/README.md, datasheet v12.
  Verified: make -C audio test PASS, A/B WAV md5 96a72789b24abe7eb9752d7b386931dd
  unchanged from 2026-09-27 (the model subtracts the bias, so the analog
  output is bit-identical); super-engine check all cases PASS (m4-audio
  through the interleaved buffer); emulator --selftest-audio (below);
  make -C apps clean.  The chip, its bench and ENGINE's logic never touch
  sample data and are unchanged.  The Smurf game port (griffin-ports) is
  told by handoff, not edited.

2026-09-29 -- psg_channels: PERIOD-255 sentinel collision fixed
  Shadow::period held 0xFF for "no PERIOD record sent yet", which is also a
  legal PERIOD (255 = 6,146 Hz), so a stream whose chosen PERIOD was 255
  never got its record and played at the reset PERIOD 0 (T8 found it on
  2026-09-28: 128 of 4,092 swept cases, 12 tone periods and 52 noise
  reloads; the Smurf trace never reached one).  Shadow::period is now
  uint16_t with NO_PERIOD = 0x100, outside the 8-bit range; VOLUME's 0xFF
  sentinel is safe (6-bit values).  T8 no longer excludes underrunning
  cases: it fails on any.  make -C audio test: 0 excluded in all four
  sweeps, PASS; A/B WAV md5 unchanged; m68k build clean.

2026-10-02 -- apps/mandelbrot: Mandelbrot explorer; mouse syscall, app vsync IRQ, whole-frame cursor chain
  A 320x240 16-colour Mandelbrot explorer in Q4.28 fixed point, driven by
  the PS/2 mouse: left click zooms in about the cursor, right click zooms
  out, q + Enter quits.  It exercises the mouse path end to end, the 4bpp
  half-rate playfield, palette cycling and a MASK cursor.

  Syscalls added (griffin.yml): MOUSE_READ 15 (mouse_poll + mouse_get_report
  into the caller's GriffinMouseReport; the firmware's mouse_report_t is now
  that same struct, in griffin_abi.h) and VIDEO_VSYNC_IRQ 16 (sets or clears
  GLUE CONFIG VSYNC_IRQ_EN while direct video is held).  ROM 130,644 bytes,
  428 free of 131,072 (was 572 free).

  Why VIDEO_VSYNC_IRQ.  A list's vblank pacers count HBLANK edges from the
  arm, so a list armed N lines after vsync shows the picture N lines low.
  Polling is fine for an app that spins on vsync (smurf, dtest); a
  compute-bound app can only poll between chunks of work thousands of cycles
  long.  sys_video_direct_start already saved and restored the level-6
  vector for exactly this, but nothing could turn the IRQ back on.  The app
  now installs its own handler (ack, write ENGINE_DESC, count the frame) and
  enables it.  griffin_abi.h states both pacing models.

  Emulator: host mouse motion, buttons and wheel over the window now drive
  the PS/2 mouse device (they were only reachable from --mouse-in).  One
  window point = one count, Y negated; --mouse-grab for relative mode;
  disabled when --mouse-in is given.  NOT verified here: it needs a window.

  Cursor: apps/lib/griffin_cursor.h.  The whole frame's cursor is one
  record chain in the vblank preamble (passthrough RUNs totalling
  y*640 + x slots, a MASK per row, RUNs between rows), so the per-line list
  is static and moving the cursor rewrites at most 125 words.  super-engine
  m12-cursor-chain: 0 of 307,200 pixels wrong at eight stress positions
  (no leading RUN, one-slot leading RUN, exactly one maximum RUN, right-edge
  wrap, bottom clip, offset row at column 638) under both render() and
  render_line_at_a_time(); negative controls one slot and one line late
  fail (68 and 46 pixels); VIDCMD FIFO high water 116 of 256.
  Rejected: a cursor drawn into the framebuffer with save-under (Brad:
  use the COMPOSITOR overlay); a per-line JIT VIDCMD packet (a fourth
  descriptor on every line, about 6% of the CPU, for nothing the chain
  does not do).

  Arithmetic: apps/mandelbrot/mandel_ref.h is the definition; mandel_iter.s
  is the 68000 loop.  Squares are two MULUs on the magnitude shifted left
  two (Al*Al dropped, result the exact floor or one less); 2xy is
  (x+y)^2 - x^2 - y^2, a third square, no sign logic.  Six MULUs per
  iteration.  Counted from instruction timings: 758 cycles worst, 662
  typical, against a 900 budget.  First design (three-MULU squares plus a
  four-MULU signed product, shifts by 12 and 4) counted 1,192 worst and was
  dropped before coding.
  `mandel selftest` in the emulator: 4,060 points, 0 mismatches against
  mandel_ref.h; 40,000 iterations in 1,740 ms of the 100 Hz tick with the
  text console displayed.  host_ref check: 0 of 76,800 pixels differ from
  double precision at the home view and at level 16.
  Render times in the emulator (its DMA cost model, not hardware): home
  view 3,870 frames (64 s), a level-1 view 4,783, a level-2 view 3,509.
  The plan's estimate of "a few minutes" was pessimistic.

  End to end, emulator screenshot against host_ref, 307,200 pixels each:
  home view; move + left click (level 1, cursor 380,200); in then out
  (back to home, cursor 180,350); two levels in with the cursor driven into
  the corner (639,479).  All four: 0 pixels differ.  That path is
  --mouse-in script -> PS2Mouse -> PORTS model -> ports_isr -> mouse.cpp ->
  SYS_MOUSE_READ -> app -> preamble -> COMPOSITOR model.
  Regression: the stored apps/dmabench C2 screenshot reproduces
  byte-identical from the OLD dmabench.bin on the new ROM.

  FINDING: GCC 15.2 m68k miscompiles small copy loops.  For
  `dst[i] = src[i]` out of a local array, with the pointer difference in a
  register, it emits
        move.w  (%a0)+, 0(%a0,%d0.l)        | d0 = dst - src, NOT adjusted
  The 68000 computes the destination after the source's post-increment, so
  every word lands two bytes high.  In mandelbrot it put the descriptor
  table one word late: ENGINE walked 1,481 one-word no-wait descriptors in
  one arm, 24 reserved-bit violations, black screen.  This is the "stack
  copy came out shifted by one word" firmware/rom.cpp recorded as not
  understood on the gc4 preamble.  apps/mandelbrot builds with
  -fno-auto-inc-dec, which removes the instruction (0 occurrences).
  The same instruction form (source (aN)+ with aN in the destination) is
  present in binaries NOT rebuilt here; each needs its index register
  checked before it is called a bug:
        firmware/rom.elf      2, both in view_gc4
        apps/smurf/smurf.elf  1, in main
        apps/exctest          1
        basic, dtest, dmabench, hello: 0
  Scan: m68k-objdump -d X.elf | perl -ne 'print if /\tmove\S*\s+%a(\d)@\+,.*%a\1\b/'
  OPEN: whether to add -fno-auto-inc-dec to firmware/ and apps/app.mk
  (ROM has 428 bytes free, so the size cost has to be measured first).

2026-10-02 -- apps/mandelbrot: keys for palette animation and palette choice; CONSOLE_RAW syscall
  Brad found the rainbow animation jarring.  `a` now toggles the palette
  animation and `p` steps through four palettes (rainbow, ochre ramp, grey
  ramp, black-and-white alternation; Mandel::PALETTES in mandel_ref.h);
  `q` quits without Enter.  Single keypresses need the firmware's raw
  keyboard path (keymap.cpp griffin_enter_raw, which read() on fd 0 already
  honours by returning scan-code set 2 bytes), so syscall CONSOLE_RAW 17
  switches it, and the loader calls griffin_leave_raw() after any app so a
  crash in raw mode cannot take the monitor with it.  ROM 130,688 bytes,
  384 free.  The app decodes F0 (break) and E0 (extended) prefixes itself.
  Verified in the emulator with --ps2-in scripts against host_ref: `pp`
  gives the grey palette and `aa` then `p` the ochre one, both 0 pixels
  different; a single `a` after 1.2 s of animation leaves the picture at
  phase 3 (72 frames / 4 = 18 steps mod 15), exactly where host_ref `phase
  3` puts it, and no other phase matches; `q` returns to the monitor and a
  following `help` line is accepted, so line editing is back.

2026-10-02 -- emulator host mouse: absolute mapping (zoom "regressed" interactively)
  Brad reported the zoom landing in the wrong place.  Scripted zooms on the
  same build are still pixel-exact against host_ref (a new
  `compare-progress` mode accepts a mid-render screenshot, so the check
  takes a 200M-cycle run instead of a finished render), so the app's zoom
  arithmetic is unchanged; what no script covers is the interactive SDL
  path.  There the free host pointer and the guest cursor drift apart
  whenever the pointer leaves the window or the guest clamps, and a click
  zooms about the guest cursor, not the pointer.  Fix: the emulator now
  keeps the guest cursor UNDER the pointer.  It tracks where a cursor that
  integrates one count per pixel from the screen centre and clamps to
  640x480 must be, and sends the delta from there to the pointer on every
  motion event.  That convention is now stated in griffin_mouse.h and the
  app follows it (initial cursor = centre + the report accumulated since
  boot).  --mouse-grab keeps the raw relative mode.  Interactive behaviour
  still unverified here (needs a window); scripted runs unchanged.

2026-10-03 -- apps/mandelbrot: fractional escape count with 2x2 ordered dither
  Brad found the integer bands read as noise where counts change fast.
  The normalised count mu = n + 2 - log2(log2 R), R = |z|^2 at escape, is
  continuous across band edges; it is now kept in quarters (two fractional
  bits) and a 2x2 Bayer dither shows the next band's colour on 0..3 of a
  cell's four pixels.  The loop records R/4 once per pixel at escape (two
  extra SQUAREs, State::norm4 at offset 16; n moved to 20) so the
  axis-escape case has a magnitude too; log2 R comes from the leading-one
  position plus a four-bit linear mantissa, log2 of that from a 64-entry
  quarter table (mandel_ref.h QUARTER_LOG2).  Everything deterministic, so
  the oracle still holds: selftest 4,060 points 0 mismatches (norm4
  compared too); host_ref check 1 of 76,800 home-view pixels differs from
  double precision (a dither threshold), 0 at level 16; emulator home view
  0 of 307,200 pixels differ, 4,291 frames (was 3,870: the two extra
  squares per pixel and the colour mapping); scripted zoom
  compare-progress 0 differ.  Rejected for now: band widening with zoom
  and adaptive supersampling (listed 2026-10-02 as follow-ons).

2026-10-03 -- COMPOSITOR LITERAL / HOLD: fit ladder (question; nothing adopted)
  vidcmd-literal-hold-proposal.md tried on the frozen pinout with the
  production flags (-preassign keep, debug, JTAG, TDI/TMS pullups,
  power_reset); rungs, benches and write-up in cpld/experiments/literal/
  (RESULTS.md).  LC / FF / Nodes+FB / PT, fitter pass:
    R0  compositor.v refit                   122/95/143/389 p2 (= shipped)
    R1  RUN_COLOR removed                    121/92/121/370 p1
    R1h R1 + HOLD(N) alone (KEEP feedback)   118/92/117/379 p1
    R2  form A literal (bank held, KEEP)     no fit, fan-in 45 on block E
    R2s form A from set_pix_value, no stall  no fit, fan-in 44
    R3/R4 R2 + HOLD, 12/10-bit counter       no fit, fan-in 45/44
    R5  form B literal (direct load, KEEP)   no fit, fan-in 41
    R5n form B without KEEP                  no fit, fan-in 41
    R6/R6n R5 + HOLD, 12/10-bit counter      no fit, fan-in 41/44
    R7  streaming RUN on R0, no literal      126/96/133/401 p1
    R8  form C into cmp_color0               no fit (grouping, routing)
    R8b form C into cmp_color1               120/92/119/386 p1  FITS
    R8bh R8b + HOLD (KEEP)                   no fit (grouping)
    R8bn R8b, 10-bit counter                 124/90/119/396 p1
    R9  R8b + streaming RUN                  no fit
    xor_synthesis on R2, R5, R5n, R8, R8bh   no fit; on R9 INTERNAL ERROR
  The limit is block E's 40-signal fan-in, not product terms.  Block E
  holds 8 of the 12 RGB_OUT pins and production already uses 39 of its 40
  signals.  Any literal colour that reaches RGB_OUT by a new per-bit path
  (staged_word, set_pix_value, or a direct D load) adds 8.  So forms A and
  B cannot fit this pinout with any lever; KEEP feedback alone costs 1.
  Form C (new; not in the proposal): the literal {00,11,colour} writes
  cmp_color1 and selects colour1, one word.  It is an ordinary one-slot
  record, so L1-L5 are unchanged and the mux gains nothing.  Costs:
  colour1 is clobbered, and there is no KEEP/HOLD (a span is LIT +
  RUN(colour1,N), N+1 px).  Margin is fitter luck: the cmp_color0 mirror
  fails.
  Seams measured on R8b: literal pitch 2 at the cadence, 1 inside a banked
  pair; RUN(pt,n) LIT RUN(pt) = a one-pixel 640-res patch at n; literal
  after MASK gap 0, and mask pixel 15 keeps the old colour1.
  The full production bench passes on every rung except its 29 RUN_COLOR
  checks (excluded before running); hand-derived lit_tb.v expectations all
  matched on the first run.  13 negative controls (cross-form, 3 mutants)
  all fail as required.
  Proposal errors found:
    form B's patch is 2 px (n-1 and n), not 1 -- the literal still occupies
      its own slot under L1;
    a form-B literal that opens a line is lost;
    "no 12-bit latch" -- cmp_color1 and set_pix_value both hold the colour;
    RUN_COLOR removal frees 3 FF / 1 LC, not ~5 FF;
    the 10-bit counter buys nothing.
  Streaming RUN fits alone (+4 LC) but not with form C.  Choosing between
  form C and streaming RUN is open (Brad).  Adoption is the five-layer list
  in RESULTS.md.

2026-10-03 -- Video: a SET(cmp_color1) stream is a literal-pixel stream on today's COMPOSITOR (finding, super-engine m13)
  With cmp_color1 on screen, each SET of it shows one 12-bit colour for
  two slots (its own slot + the hold behind it); hand-derived slot maps
  matched the model on every trace and on a 16-pixel sprite band over a
  4bpp playfield.  Rules: lead-in [SET c1, RUN(c1,2)] behind a record of
  >= 3 slots; one word a pixel, plus one invisible pair word after every
  record of >= 3 slots; exit RUN(pass) lands with no stale slot; a hole of
  p >= 2 pixels is RUN(pass,2p-1) + invisible SET + RUN; a ONE-pixel hole
  needs its following colour already in a register; x = 0 and x = 2 are
  not exact.  A 16-pixel row is 18 words; delivery needs ~10 of them
  buffered, so the program goes first in its hblank or a line ahead.  A
  late word shifts everything after it (the stream supplies the timing).
  Compared with the fit ladder of the same day: form C LITERAL (fits,
  120/128) is this stream with the select built in -- no lead-in, true
  one-pixel holes and patches -- at the cost of RUN_COLOR.  Write-up:
  super-engine/SET-STREAM.md.
  Addendum (same day, all pins free; RESULTS.md "Part 2"): with the
  //PIN block removed (free placement, production flags otherwise), LC /
  FF / Nodes+FB / PT, pass, tightest block fan-in:
    R2  form A literal                   123/93/120/412 p1, 34/40
    R3  form A + HOLD, 12-bit counter    no fit (grouping)
    R4  form A + HOLD, 10-bit counter    114/91/124/381 p2, 35
    R2st form A + streaming              126/94/141/392 p2, 38
    R4st form A + HOLD + streaming       118/92/119/357 p1, 35
         (streaming = count bits 11:10 = 10; HOLD = 0x)
    R5, R6, R6n form B with KEEP         no fit even free; xor no help
    R5n form B without KEEP              118 p1, four blocks at 40/40
    R2s/R4s stall-free form A (+HOLD)    123 p1 / 120 p1; +streaming no fit
  Free placement moves 55-59 of 62 pins.  Runs made before the scope
  change found less: swapping RGB_OUT 3/0/1/2 with VIDCMD_Q 10/11/8/13
  (block G) fits R4st at 118 p1, tightest 34, moving 8 pins.  Releasing
  only RGB_OUT under keep makes the fitter refuse.
  lit_tb (new cases STREAM10, FORM_BN, FORM_A2) passed on the first run
  against hand derivations.  The production bench shows 0 unexpected
  failures on every fitting rung; all negative controls fail.
  Correction to the entry above: R2s ("stall-free form A") is not form A.
  It is a one-slot literal that lands adjacent in a banked pair; its
  first claimed simulation had not been run.
  Answer: form A (+ HOLD + streaming) fits a re-pinned COMPOSITOR with
  10 LC spare.  Form B as proposed fits no pinout tried.
